Now update the evaluate() implementation in the cpp. Key changes:

1. Generation bump reset: also reset stall_reported_level and degraded_reported.
2. Stall rule: only emit when `state.consecutive_stalls == threshold` (first crossing) OR severity escalates to CRITICAL (`consecutive >= 2*threshold` and last reported level < 2).
3. Degraded rule: emit only when first crossing since last recovery (`!degraded_reported`).
4. Recovery resets the flags.

Current stall code:
```cpp
if (config_.stall_timeout_ms > 0) {
    if (!has_traffic) {
        state.consecutive_stalls++;
        if (state.consecutive_stalls >= config_.stall_consecutive_windows) {
            // build anomaly ... severity CRITICAL if >= 2*threshold
            anomalies.push_back(...);
        }
    } else {
        state.consecutive_stalls = 0;
        state.last_active_ts_ms = now_ms;
    }
}
```

New:
```cpp
if (config_.stall_timeout_ms > 0) {
    if (!has_traffic) {
        state.consecutive_stalls++;
        if (state.consecutive_stalls >= config_.stall_consecutive_windows) {
            const int level = (state.consecutive_stalls >= config_.stall_consecutive_windows * 2) ? 2 : 1;
            if (level > state.stall_reported_level) {
                state.stall_reported_level = level;
                // build + push anomaly
            }
        }
    } else {
        state.consecutive_stalls = 0;
        state.stall_reported_level = 0;
        state.last_active_ts_ms = now_ms;
    }
}
```

Degraded:
```cpp
if (config_.min_expected_bps > 0.0) {
    if (has_traffic && total_bps < config_.min_expected_bps) {
        state.consecutive_degraded++;
        if (state.consecutive_degraded >= config_.degraded_consecutive_windows && !state.degraded_reported) {
            state.degraded_reported = true;
            // push
        }
    } else if (has_traffic) {
        state.consecutive_degraded = 0;
        state.degraded_reported = false;
    }
}
```

Also generation reset adds the two flags reset.

Wait — one subtlety with the AttributionLost rule: it also emits every window while scope stays "controller". Should it be gated too? Yes — same flooding concern. Add `bool attribution_reported{false}` reset on scope change/device attribution. Gate: emit only when scope != "device" AND has_traffic AND !attribution_reported; reset when device attributed (scope == "device") or traffic stops.

Note generation reset also covers device changes.

